---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Why Rust — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="I had to decide: write all of the app in Rust, or put a web or Swift interface on a Rust engine. Here is what I compared and what made the decision."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-06-28">June 28, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Why Rust</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          I had to decide: write all of the app in Rust, or put a web or Swift
          interface on a Rust engine. Here is what I compared and what made the
          decision.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 5 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          I had two options: all of the app in Rust, or a fast Rust engine
          with a better interface in React or Swift. I went with all Rust. For
          this, I accepted a less attractive interface and weaker accessibility
          (support for screen readers and similar tools). A photo editor must
          respond immediately to each mouse movement. When two languages must
          stay synchronized that frequently, bugs usually occur in the handoff
          between them. Coding agents have difficulty with these bugs, so I
          must find them by hand.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Write down the trade-offs before you pick.</strong> A list of
          the gains and losses of each option showed me what I did not know
          yet. Thus I could answer these questions first, and they did not
          surprise me later.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Pick a design that you can debug yourself.</strong> Agents
          write almost all of this code, and they usually write it well.
          Sometimes an agent fixes one symptom after another and does not find
          the cause. Then I need to step in and find the problem myself, so the
          design must be simple.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Plan for the next one or two steps.</strong> Swift is a good
          choice for a Mac-only app. But I wanted a browser version, and that
          ruled Swift out right away.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Keep the core separate from the UI.</strong> I use one
          language, but I keep the photo editing logic in its own module. Thus
          I can replace the interface later.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why I considered Rust</h2>
      <p>
        I think that Rust is a very interesting language. It is roughly
        as powerful as C++, and its compiler is strict. The compiler does not
        build code that looks unsafe. It feels a bit like a picky reviewer who
        sits next to me.
      </p>
      <p>
        But this does not automatically make Rust correct for a photo editor.
        Before I wrote much code, I had many questions that I could not answer.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>Is Rust a good choice for this app?</li>
        <li>
          Should I write only the critical part, the image engine, in Rust, and
          make the UI with a tool such as React?
        </li>
        <li>Or should I build all of the app in Rust?</li>
        <li>Does Rust have a UI library that is good enough to use?</li>
        <li>
          Or should I write the UI in Swift, so that it feels like a native Mac app?
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Two more things worried me. I wanted a browser version one day, so I could edit in the same way without my Mac. I also
        thought that one day I could want support for Windows and Linux.
      </p>

      <p>In the end, I had two ways to build the app.</p>

      <figure class="lp-arch">
        <div class="lp-arch-panel">
          <p class="lp-arch-label">Option 1 &middot; All Rust</p>
          <div class="lp-arch-process">
            <span class="lp-arch-process-name">One Rust app</span>
            <div class="lp-arch-box">UI module</div>
            <div class="lp-arch-link">function calls</div>
            <div class="lp-arch-box lp-arch-box-core">Core engine</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-arch-panel">
          <p class="lp-arch-label">Option 2 &middot; Split</p>
          <div class="lp-arch-process">
            <span class="lp-arch-process-name">Electron</span>
            <div class="lp-arch-box">React UI</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lp-arch-link lp-arch-link-cross">
            events &darr;&ensp;pixels &uarr;
          </div>
          <div class="lp-arch-process">
            <span class="lp-arch-process-name">Rust</span>
            <div class="lp-arch-box lp-arch-box-core">Core engine</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          With all Rust, the interface calls the engine directly. With the
          split design, each slider drag and each preview must move between two
          separate programs.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>The cost of each option</h2>
      <p>
        All Rust gives me one language for all of the app. I do not have to
        copy image data between two programs. I also do not have to send each
        click and drag across a bridge between them. The app is also faster,
        needs fewer external libraries, and is simpler to ship.
      </p>
      <p>
        This option also has real disadvantages. Rust has no official UI
        toolkit. The libraries that people made work. But my UI will not look
        as good as a well-styled web page or a native Mac app.
        Browsers also give good accessibility by default, and the Rust UI
        libraries are far behind.
      </p>
      <p>
        A split design lets me use the best tools on each side. CSS lets me
        adjust each pixel, so I have full control of the appearance. The cost
        is that I must keep two systems synchronized.
      </p>

      <h2>The Swift option</h2>
      <p>
        A Swift interface feels the most natural on a Mac, and I wanted that.
        But it has the same split as option 2: Swift communicates with a Rust
        engine. Also, Swift does not run in the browser. A browser version
        (made with WebAssembly, which lets compiled code run in a web page)
        needs a second interface, written from nothing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Swift also makes me dependent on Apple. Windows or Linux will need one
        more interface. The Rust UI libraries and Electron already run on all
        three.
      </p>

      <h2>Comparison</h2>
      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="lp-compare-pick">All Rust</th>
              <th scope="col">Rust + React</th>
              <th scope="col">Rust + Swift</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Languages</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">1</td>
              <td>2</td>
              <td>2</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Engine and UI to keep synchronized</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">No</td>
              <td>Yes</td>
              <td>Yes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Extra runtime to ship</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">None</td>
              <td>Electron</td>
              <td>None</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Appearance</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Plain</td>
              <td>Anything CSS can do</td>
              <td>Native Mac</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Accessibility</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Limited</td>
              <td>Browser-grade</td>
              <td>Native Mac</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Browser (WASM) version</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Same code</td>
              <td>Engine to WASM, UI as is</td>
              <td>Needs a second UI</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Windows and Linux</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Same code</td>
              <td>Same code</td>
              <td>Needs another UI</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        In a web app, people expect a short delay. You click a button, you
        wait a moment, and the server answers. A photo editor is different,
        because drag, zoom, and pan operations are continuous. Each mouse
        movement must go to the engine and come back as a new image. This
        occurs sixty times a second, without a delay that you can feel.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 220" role="img" aria-label="One slider drag, sixty times a second. With all Rust, the slider moves, an edit function runs, and new pixels appear. There is no runtime boundary. With a Rust engine and a web UI, the same drag crosses a runtime boundary into the engine and again on the return. This occurs two times for each frame.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">ONE SLIDER DRAG, 60 TIMES A SECOND</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="24" width="640" height="88" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="44">All Rust</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="16" y="56" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="104" y="76" text-anchor="middle">Slider moves</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="192" y1="71" x2="214" y2="71"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M214 66 L224 71 L214 76 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="224" y="56" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="312" y="76" text-anchor="middle">Edit function</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="400" y1="71" x2="422" y2="71"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M422 66 L432 71 L422 76 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="432" y="56" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="520" y="76" text-anchor="middle">New pixels</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="104">No runtime boundary. It is a function call.</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="124" width="640" height="88" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="144">Rust engine, web UI</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="16" y="156" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="104" y="176" text-anchor="middle">Slider moves (JS)</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" x1="208" y1="152" x2="208" y2="190" stroke-dasharray="3 4"></line>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="224" y="156" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="312" y="176" text-anchor="middle">Engine (Rust)</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" x1="416" y1="152" x2="416" y2="190" stroke-dasharray="3 4"></line>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="432" y="156" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="520" y="176" text-anchor="middle">New pixels (JS)</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="16" y="204">Two boundary crosses per frame, 120 a second.</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          The Rust engine is equally fast in the split version. The extra time
          comes from the data that crosses between the two sides two times in
          each frame.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        To keep two systems synchronized at that rate is much more difficult
        than to handle an occasional request. When they are not synchronized,
        the bug is usually in the handoff between them. Neither side's code
        looks wrong on its own. This type of bug is very
        difficult to find.
      </p>

      <h2>Why this was more important for me than usual</h2>
      <p>
        This part made the decision for me.
      </p>
      <p>
        I did not write most of this code. Coding agents wrote it. In my
        experience, they are strong when all of the code is in one language.
        They are much weaker when they must find a small mismatch between two
        systems in different languages.
      </p>
      <p>
        I wanted a design where I can step in and find the problem myself
        when the agents get stuck. With one language and one program, there is no handoff between
        systems where this type of bug can occur.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second reason was performance. I want this app to be fast and to
        use only the memory that it needs. With all of the code in Rust, there
        is no second runtime to load. Also, the app does not send copies of the
        image between two sides.
      </p>

      <h2>How I keep the option to change</h2>
      <p>
        I use one language, but I keep the UI logic and the core photo editing
        in separate modules. If I change my decision in a year and want a
        different interface, I can keep most of the core without changes.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the same reason, I clean up sloppy generated code as it
        arrives. I do not let it pile up. I want the code to stay small enough
        that I can still understand all of it.
      </p>


